{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210729,"nonce":"RPYUKh2auKWo8aGq6uXggRWQrwM=","title":"Tiered-mode Null.toBe flake: two root causes","message":"Fixed in commit 365d61f. Record migrated from session memory 2026-09-12.\n\nThe long-running tiered-mode flake (\"Null does not implement\n'toBe(_)'\") that affected hatch-crypto / random / proc / json /\nurl was resolved in commit 365d61f (2026-04-22). Two unrelated\nroot causes stacked on top of each other.\n\n**Root cause 1 — wrong module context at JIT entry:**\nWhen the interpreter dispatched into a JIT-compiled callee, two\npaths (vm_interp leaf fast path + dispatch_closure_bc_inner) left\n`ctx.module_vars` / `ctx.module_name` pointing at the **caller's**\nmodule. The JIT-compiled callee's `GetModuleVar @N` then read\ncaller-module-slot-N, misresolving cross-module class references.\n\n**Why:** The Phase-1 stdlib is heavily cross-module (specs in one\npackage call `Expect.that` from @hatch:assert), so any hot loop\nthat tier-ups and calls into @hatch:assert would misresolve the\n`Assertion` class and silently return Null.\n\n**How to apply:** if a regression ever shows this symptom again,\nfirst check whether `module_vars` is properly swapped at every\nJIT entry point (`call_jit_with_shadow`, the vm_interp leaf fast\npath, `dispatch_closure_bc_inner`, and any other path that calls\na raw JIT `fn_ptr`).\n\n**Root cause 2 — wren_cmp_eq used bitwise u64 compare:**\n`wren_cmp_eq(a, b)` was `Value::bool(a == b)` — correct for Num /\nBool / Null, wrong for heap objects. Specifically, `ObjString`\nequality in Wren is content-based, but every `s[i]` subscript\nallocates a fresh ObjString, so JIT'd `s[i] == \":\"` always\nreturned false after tier-up.\n\n**Why:** A simple oversight — `Value::equals` already handled all\nthree cases (identical bits, numeric NaN semantics, string\ncontent) but the JIT's cmp_eq runtime helper skipped it.\n\n**How to apply:** any runtime helper that compares two `Value`\nbits should call `Value::from_bits(a).equals(Value::from_bits(b))`\ninstead of raw `==`. Grep for `a == b` inside `wren_cmp_*` /\nother JIT runtime helpers if something else surfaces.\n\n**Follow-up: perf** — the module swap on every JIT-into-JIT call\nregressed tight method-dispatch microbenchmarks (method_call,\nbinary_trees). Commit 3391db9 adds an intra-module fast path:\ncompare the callee's module_name byte-pointer+len to the current\nctx; skip the lookup + context write when equal. Same-module\ncalls (recursive, same-class) hit zero overhead; cross-module\ncalls (the case the original fix targeted) still pay the swap.\nBenchmarks back to pre-fix noise band.\n\n**Known not-fixed by this:** `hatch-http` still fails in tiered.\nInterpreter 25/25, pure `--mode jit` 25/25, tiered 0/5 — classic\ninterpreter→JIT transition bug but separate from the pair fixed\nhere. Symptom: `p[\"headers\"]` returns Null on a Map that visibly\nHAS the key (`p.count == 1`, `p.body` shows the correct JSON).\nCrashes with \"unsupported: subscript get with arity 1\" when the\ncaller chains a second subscript on the Null result.\n\n**What's been ruled out** (2026-04-23 investigation):\n1. `MapKey` hash/eq — both content-based for strings; stable\n   under relocation.\n2. `update_pointers_in_object_inline` for ObjMap — drain+update+\n   reinsert is correct. Instrumented with size-diff trace over a\n   full http.spec run; before/drained/after always match.\n3. `map_keys` + `alloc_list` — `VM::new_list` `push_jit_root`s\n   every element before allocation, re-reads via `jit_root_at`\n   afterward. GC during list materialization can't stale the\n   Values.\n4. `promote_typed::\u003cObjMap\u003e` — ptr::read transfers HashMap heap\n   ownership cleanly; old nursery location marked FORWARDED and\n   not dropped.\n5. Network is not the problem — httpbin is reachable, body\n   arrives correctly (body.count == 230, preview looks right).\n\n**Still open suspects:**\n- `wren_subscript_set`'s write-barrier ordering for Maps. It\n  inserts into `map.entries` BEFORE calling write_barrier on the\n  key/value. If a GC fires between the insert and the barrier\n  call, the fresh MapKey slot is populated with a pre-GC pointer\n  AND the Map isn't yet in the remembered set, so\n  `update_old_gen_pointers_inline` might skip it. Fix candidate:\n  move the write_barrier BEFORE the insert.\n- HashMap entry state when both Map and key are promoted in the\n  same minor cycle. Could be order-sensitive.\n\n**How to resume:** write a minimal Wren test that forces\nmap-set → allocate-until-GC → map-get in a tight loop, build\nwith ASan, and run under tiered. That surfaces stale-pointer\nreads directly, avoiding the \"Map looks right but lookup fails\"\nindirection.","files":null}]}